WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.152
This lesson is not mainly about memorizing several equations and hoping one feels familiar.

2
00:00:06.152 --> 00:00:12.304
It is about checking the motion story, confirming constant acceleration, listing the known quantities, naming the unknown, and then selecting the most direct relation.

3
00:00:12.304 --> 00:00:18.457
When learners do that consistently, equation choice becomes orderly instead of stressful.

4
00:00:18.457 --> 00:00:24.609
The formulas stop feeling like a wall and start behaving like tools.

5
00:00:24.609 --> 00:00:30.761
Keep this takeaway in view: Equation choice is a reasoning step, not a memory lottery.

6
00:00:30.761 --> 00:00:36.913
Condition means constant acceleration.

7
00:00:36.913 --> 00:00:43.066
The motion story must match one steady acceleration.

8
00:00:43.066 --> 00:00:49.218
Method means knowns and unknown.

9
00:00:49.218 --> 00:00:55.370
Choose the equation that links what you have to what you need.

10
00:00:55.370 --> 00:01:02.940
The forecast-board graph matters because it shows one steady slope, which means one steady acceleration.

11
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:10.510
That is the condition under which the standard motion equations work cleanly.

12
00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:18.080
If the acceleration were changing unpredictably, these compact equations would no longer describe the whole story properly.

13
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:25.650
So before using an equation, the learner should always ask: does this motion really match the constant-acceleration model?

14
00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:33.220
Keep this takeaway in view: Use the equations only when the motion story matches the constant-acceleration condition.

15
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:40.790
Straight-line clue: One steady slope signals one steady acceleration.

16
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:48.360
Permission: That is what makes the constant-acceleration equations trustworthy here.

17
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.178
The mission-kit analogy helps because it makes equation choice procedural.

18
00:01:54.178 --> 00:01:59.996
If you need to tighten one bolt, you choose the correct spanner, not every tool in the box.

19
00:01:59.996 --> 00:02:05.813
In the same way, if u, a, and t are known and v is required, you choose the relation that links those four quantities directly.

20
00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:11.631
The best equation is the one that reaches the answer with the fewest unnecessary detours and with the constant-acceleration condition still respected.

21
00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:17.449
Keep this takeaway in view: Equation choice works best when it is treated like tool selection, not lucky recall.

22
00:02:17.449 --> 00:02:23.267
Knowns means what you already have.

23
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:29.084
These are the quantities supplied by the question.

24
00:02:29.084 --> 00:02:34.902
Unknown means what you need.

25
00:02:34.902 --> 00:02:40.720
This is the target quantity the tool must reach.

26
00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:47.899
Several standard equations appear in this lesson, but each one is useful for a different question shape.

27
00:02:47.899 --> 00:02:55.078
V equals u plus a t is the direct route when the final velocity is needed from initial velocity, acceleration, and time.

28
00:02:55.078 --> 00:03:02.257
S equals u t plus one-half a t squared is direct when displacement is needed from initial velocity, acceleration, and time.

29
00:03:02.257 --> 00:03:09.436
S equals open bracket u plus v close bracket over two times t is strong when average velocity is the natural bridge.

30
00:03:09.436 --> 00:03:16.614
The art is choosing rather than collecting.

31
00:03:16.614 --> 00:03:23.793
Keep this takeaway in view: Do not memorize blindly; match the equation board to the question shape.

32
00:03:23.793 --> 00:03:30.972
Need v: v = u + at is often the clean first choice when u, a, and t are known.

33
00:03:30.972 --> 00:03:38.151
Need s: s = ut + 1/2at^2 is direct when u, a, and t are known.

34
00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:45.330
Pick from the knowns and the unknown after confirming constant acceleration.

35
00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:50.220
A dependable routine is this.

36
00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:55.110
First, check the condition: is the acceleration constant?

37
00:03:55.110 --> 00:04:00.000
Second, list the known quantities with units and signs.

38
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.890
Third, mark the unknown clearly.

39
00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:09.780
Then choose the equation that uses those knowns and reaches that unknown with no unnecessary extra variable.

40
00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.670
Learners who do this consistently make far fewer substitution mistakes because the equation has already been justified before the arithmetic starts.

41
00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.560
Keep this takeaway in view: Condition, inventory, target: this is the decision routine for the whole lesson.

42
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.450
Condition means constant a?.

43
00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:29.340
The equations belong only to one steady acceleration story.

44
00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.230
Inventory means what is known?.

45
00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:39.120
List u, v, a, t, or s before picking the relation.

46
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.174
A common trap is to start substituting into the first equation that comes to mind.

47
00:04:45.174 --> 00:04:51.228
That often creates a second unknown or forces unnecessary extra work.

48
00:04:51.228 --> 00:04:57.281
Another trap is to forget to check whether acceleration is constant at all.

49
00:04:57.281 --> 00:05:03.335
The safer habit is slower at the beginning and faster overall: confirm the condition, list the knowns, mark the unknown, then choose the direct relation.

50
00:05:03.335 --> 00:05:09.389
That way the substitution step becomes almost automatic.

51
00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:15.442
Keep this takeaway in view: A justified equation choice saves time and prevents avoidable algebra detours.

52
00:05:15.442 --> 00:05:21.496
Check the constant-acceleration condition first.

53
00:05:21.496 --> 00:05:27.550
List the knowns and the unknown before choosing the equation.

54
00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.400
Ask three quick questions.

55
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.250
What condition must hold before the standard motion equations are used?

56
00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.100
Constant acceleration.

57
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:42.950
What should be listed before the equation is chosen?

58
00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:46.800
The known quantities and the unknown.

59
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.650
And if u, a, and t are known while v is required, which equation is the direct first choice?

60
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:54.500
V equals u plus a t.

61
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:58.350
Those three checks are the spine of the lesson.

62
00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:02.200
Keep this takeaway in view: The equation board becomes manageable once the choice routine is secure.

63
00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:06.050
Condition means constant acceleration.

64
00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.900
This is the permission check for the equation set.

65
00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.750
Before choosing means knowns and unknown.

66
00:06:13.750 --> 00:06:17.600
The equation is chosen from the question data, not from habit.

67
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:23.382
You are ready for the worked example now.

68
00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:29.165
The trolley starts at three metres per second and accelerates steadily at two metres per second squared for four seconds.

69
00:06:29.165 --> 00:06:34.947
First check the condition: the acceleration is steady, so the constant-acceleration equations are allowed.

70
00:06:34.947 --> 00:06:40.729
The knowns are u equals three, a equals two, and t equals four.

71
00:06:40.729 --> 00:06:46.512
The unknown is v.

72
00:06:46.512 --> 00:06:52.294
So choose the direct relation v equals u plus a t.

73
00:06:52.294 --> 00:06:58.076
Substitute the values: v equals three plus two times four, which gives eleven metres per second.

74
00:06:58.076 --> 00:07:03.858
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: choose first, substitute second, interpret all the way through.

75
00:07:03.858 --> 00:07:09.641
Step 1: Confirm that the motion story uses constant acceleration.

76
00:07:09.641 --> 00:07:15.423
Step 2: List the knowns, identify the unknown, and choose the direct relation.

77
00:07:15.423 --> 00:07:21.205
knowns: u = 3, a = 2, t = 4.

78
00:07:21.205 --> 00:07:26.988
choose v = u + at.

79
00:07:26.988 --> 00:07:32.770
Equation choice came from the knowns, the unknown, and the constant-acceleration condition.
